1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,321
Where does a payment system start, if not with payments?

2
00:00:03,321 --> 00:00:08,238
It starts with a project that builds, and an environment that runs with one command.

3
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:14,452
We use Java twenty-five and Spring Boot four, built by one Gradle build with a module for each part of the domain.

4
00:00:14,452 --> 00:00:22,250
The app has five modules, partner, commission, terminal, card, and payment, and each one owns its own database schema.

5
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:28,666
A boundary test fails the build if one module reaches into another module's code or tables.

6
00:00:28,666 --> 00:00:34,500
Every version is pinned in one catalog file, so the build is the same on every machine.

7
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:40,380
Docker Compose starts Postgres, Redis, and Keycloak, and nothing else yet.

8
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:44,583
Every other tool joins only in the episode whose problem needs it.

9
00:00:44,583 --> 00:00:49,523
The app logs in JSON and reports its health, so we can see it from the very first minute.

10
00:00:49,523 --> 00:00:52,011
One command, and the whole world is up.

11
00:00:52,011 --> 00:00:55,488
Next, we design the domain before we write a single table.

